- Confirm the skill is listed in the tab, and that `herdr agent get` shows `"focused": false` for both tabs.
- Two Bash calls, both background, sent together:

```bash
herdr agent prompt prod-codex "$(cat <scratch>/brief-T<n>-r1-codex.txt)" --wait --timeout 2700000; \
herdr agent read prod-codex --source recent-unwrapped --lines 200 > <inbox>/prod-codex.last
```

  The same call goes to `prod-fable` with its own brief. Then I update the backlog and republish.

## Why

- **A new task enters at stage 0.** The backlog entry comes first, and no executor exists until the brainstorm gate passes.
- **The missing skill is the operator's decision.** Your ruling requires both model families to run `superpowers:brainstorming`. Installing a CLI plugin changes your workstation, which is one of the four decisions you keep, so it is a poll with my recommendation first, not a vote and not my call.
- **I do not start prod-fable alone.** Round 1 must be two independent drafts. A head start would get one family's questions balloted first and hand them to prod-codex as already decided. A lone draft would also become a single-advisor brainstorm by default, which is a waiver only you can grant.
- **No substitute.** Letting Codex read the skill's markdown is not the skill.
- **"Start today" is met.** The task is entered and stage 0 is open; the only blocker is one poll answer.
- **The brief carries no design hints.** It holds facts, paths and an explicit "not verified" line.
- **One discrepancy.** Manifest §15 records the Codex plugin as installed today, but the workstation shows otherwise. I trust the check and re-verify in the tab before dispatch.

Operator-facing texts are in English here because of this session's language setting; your standing ruling says Russian.
